  "worked_solution": "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $2$ trapezoids. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14251762/assets/8a7521b862c6533554df3931b03f7aa4b6b669167ced130e4bb353605fd24ea2.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =4 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-4+6 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{4,0,6\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=4.$",
